[  173.2s] NOTE assumption: The memo keeps the 30% floor (rule 17) and the limit of two wrong identity answers in total (rule 9).
[  173.2s] NOTE assumption: Floor = 30% of arrears_amount, rounded up to the next whole pound. A result that is already a whole number of pounds is not rounded further. Examples: £100.00 gives £30; £101.00 gives £30.30, which becomes £31. monthly_payment is never used or quoted.
[  173.2s] NOTE assumption: If the rounded-up floor would be more than arrears_amount (only possible for very small debts), the floor is capped at the full arrears_amount, because rule 18 only accepts amounts between the floor and arrears_amount.
[  173.2s] NOTE assumption: get_account gives amounts in pounds (amount_scale 1). create_promise takes whole pence (promise_scale 100, so £87.50 is sent as 8750).
[  173.2s] NOTE assumption: Rule 20 counts the opening ask (full amount today) as attempt 1, so opening_is_attempt is true. The early band's one attempt is the opening ask alone. The mid band adds step 2, and the late band adds steps 2 and 3.
[  173.2s] NOTE assumption: With two products, a flat no to 'pay both in full today' counts as step 1 for each product (rule 23).
[  173.2s] NOTE assumption: Ladder step 2 is the full amount on the 5th business day after today, or on the deadline if that is earlier. The engine is assumed to clamp offer dates to the product's deadline.
[  173.2s] NOTE assumption: Rule 21: if the customer says the full amount is too much, the next step offers the floor on that step's date and uses an attempt. This is modelled as too_much_floor 'attempt_1' with ladder step 2 set to full_or_floor.
